      "explain": "A <strong>presentation</strong> is a structured spoken delivery to an audience, usually with visual support, made to inform, persuade or train. Good presentations follow a process: <strong>plan → analyse the audience → structure → prepare visuals → rehearse → deliver → handle questions</strong>.",
      "keyPoints": [
        "<strong>Step 1 — Define the purpose:</strong> to inform, to persuade or to train?",
        "<strong>Step 2 — Analyse the audience:</strong> what do they know, want, and have time for?",
        "<strong>Step 3 — Collect and select content:</strong> keep only what serves the purpose.",
        "<strong>Step 4 — Structure it:</strong> Introduction → Body → Conclusion.",
        "<strong>Step 5 — Prepare visuals</strong> — after the content, never before.",
        "<strong>Step 6 — Rehearse aloud</strong>, with a timer, at least three times.",
        "<strong>Step 7 — Deliver:</strong> eye contact, pace, pauses, movement, no reading.",
        "<strong>Step 8 — Handle questions</strong> calmly at the end.",
        "Classic rule: <em>tell them what you will say, say it, then tell them what you said.</em>",
        "Arrive early and check the projector, cable, sound and your file."
