      "subtopics": [
        {
          "title": "The three-part structure",
          "text": "<strong>Introduction (10–15%):</strong> greet, introduce yourself, state the topic, say why it matters and preview your agenda. <strong>Body (70–80%):</strong> three to five main points, each with evidence or an example, in a logical order. <strong>Conclusion (10–15%):</strong> summarise the main points, state your key message or recommendation, thank the audience and invite questions."
        },
        {
          "title": "Rehearsal is not optional",
          "text": "Rehearse <strong>aloud and standing</strong>, not silently in your head — the two feel completely different. Time yourself; almost everyone overruns. Record one rehearsal and watch it, however uncomfortable that is. Rehearse the <strong>opening two minutes</strong> until you can do them automatically, because those are the minutes your nerves are highest."
        },
        {
          "title": "Handling questions",
          "text": "Listen to the whole question without interrupting. Pause, then repeat or paraphrase it so the whole room hears it. Answer briefly and honestly. If you do not know, say so: '<strong>I don't have that figure — I will find out and get back to you</strong>' is respected; bluffing is not. If a questioner is hostile, stay calm and answer the content, not the tone."
        },
        {
          "title": "Timing and the clock",
          "text": "Never plan to use the whole slot. For a 10-minute slot, prepare 8 minutes of content, because live delivery and questions always expand. Keep roughly one slide per minute as a rough guide. Finishing early is professional; being cut off mid-sentence by the moderator is not."
