          "text": "Being watched by a group triggers the same ancient 'fight or flight' response as physical danger. Adrenaline raises the heart rate, tightens the throat and diverts blood from the hands, which is exactly why the mouth goes dry and the hands shake. Understanding that it is <strong>chemistry, not character</strong>, already removes some of the shame that makes it worse."
        },
        {
          "title": "Before you speak",
          "text": "Rehearse aloud and standing. Sleep properly the night before. Arrive early and stand where you will speak, so the space feels familiar. Avoid excess caffeine. Do slow, deep breathing for two minutes — long exhales physically slow the heart rate. Then focus on your first sentence, which should be so well rehearsed that it needs no thought at all."
        },
        {
          "title": "While you are speaking",
          "text": "Start slower than feels natural. Find two or three friendly faces and speak to them. Plant your feet; do not sway. If your mind blanks, <strong>pause and look at your notes</strong> — three seconds of silence feels like a minute to you and like nothing to the audience. If your voice shakes, take one breath and continue; it settles within about thirty seconds."
        },
        {
