      "examAnswer": "Visual aids are supporting materials such as slides, charts, graphs, images, videos, models and handouts that are used to make an oral presentation clearer, more interesting and easier to remember. Effective slide design follows the principle of one idea per slide and the 6x6 rule, which limits a slide to about six bullet points of about six words each. The text should be in a plain sans-serif font of at least twenty-eight points, with strong contrast between text and background, a limited and consistent colour scheme, and charts or images in preference to long passages of text. Common multimedia tools include Microsoft PowerPoint, Google Slides, Canva and Prezi for slides, video and screen recordings for demonstration, and platforms such as Zoom, Google Meet and Microsoft Teams for online delivery. The most common error is reading directly from the slides, which causes the speaker to turn away from the audience and reduces the presentation to a recitation; slides should therefore carry only keywords while the explanation is given orally.",
